  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/RgDCTIWJmAmtP06Wgn3PEn">
    <skos:example>In an injury case, a witness could testify about hearing plaintiff cry out after the accident -- statements like “Call for help!” -- where the statement demonstrates that the plaintiff was conscious.</skos:example>
    <skos:example>In a defamation case, a witness can testify: “Smith said that Jones was a socialist.”</skos:example>
    <skos:example>In a contract case, the witness can testify to this verbal act: “I heard Smith say: ‘I will sell you Property X if you sell me Property Y.’”</skos:example>
    <skos:definition>Verbal Acts, also known as "operative words," are statements that have legal significance or create legal consequences independently of the truth of the content asserted in the statement. These statements are not considered hearsay because their relevance lies in the fact that they were made, rather than in the truthfulness of the content.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/RDgZFYp1iCuW1Tod7uoMWjE"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label>Verbal Act</rdfs:label>
    <skos:prefLabel>Operative Words</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
