  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/R9e8PXI2IEusTTC9xKXN7hA">
    <skos:example>Plaintiff is adverse to Defendant</skos:example>
    <skos:example>Contractual Party A is adverse to Contractual Party B</skos:example>
    <rdfs:label>isAdverseTo</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:label>sali:isAdverseTo</rdfs:label>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <skos:altLabel>is adverse to</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition>In legal terminology, "is adverse to" describes a relationship characterized by opposition, conflict, or differing interests between parties in a legal context. This typically applies to situations where one party's interests, claims, or actions are directly opposed to those of another party, as in litigation, contractual disputes, or criminal proceedings.</skos:definition>
    <skos:notation>folio:isAdverseTo</skos:notation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/RD1Ta5Qsm3Sa3jZjn0f9zxP">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/Rb9ymr4tGQwciL1lrwg0UN"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label>Trademark Search Process</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition>The trademark search process is a systematic examination conducted to determine whether a proposed trademark is available for use and registration. This process involves checking databases, registers, and other sources to identify any existing trademarks that may conflict with the proposed mark.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
